Goal: Read the text actually visible in the image, preserve its meaning and ordering, and speak only what the OCR system has genuinely recognised.

### What accuracy should mean

- Character accuracy: Correctly recognise individual letters, numbers and symbols.
- Exact words: Avoid substitutions such as reading `18` as `16`, or changing a name or product label.
- Punctuation and formatting: Preserve punctuation and useful line breaks. Maintain reading order for paragraphs, lists, columns and tables where supported.
- Small and difficult text: Test small fonts, low contrast, glare, curved packaging, angled pages, blur and partially obstructed text.
- Multiple text regions: Detect and read separate visible text regions in a sensible order without accidentally merging unrelated lines.
- Faithful speech: The spoken result must match the recognised text. The language model or text-to-speech component must not silently rewrite, complete or correct uncertain words.
- No guessing: If a character is unclear, report the uncertainty or ask the user to reposition the camera. Never invent a missing digit.
- Useful image enhancement: Use OpenCV 5 preprocessing, such as contrast enhancement or deskewing, only when it improves recognition. Keep the original image available because preprocessing can sometimes destroy text details.

### Recommended measurable targets

| Metric                     | Suggested target                                                                                  |
| -------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Character error rate (CER) | At most 2% on clear, supported printed text                                                       |
| Word error rate (WER)      | At most 5% on clear, supported printed text                                                       |
| Reading-order accuracy     | At least 95% on a defined set of layouts                                                          |
| Spoken-text fidelity       | No differences from the selected OCR output, except explicitly disclosed formatting normalisation |
| Response time              | Aim for a first readable result within 0.5–1.5 seconds for ordinary short text                    |

CER and WER must be measured against ground-truth transcripts. Targets for clear printed text should not be presented as expected performance on handwriting, tiny text or severely blurred images; those require separate evaluations.

For important information such as medication labels, addresses, account numbers and dates, the app should be particularly cautious about uncertain characters.

