Goal: Produce a concise, useful description of the actual scene, including relevant objects, their approximate spatial relationships and visible details, without making things up.

### What accuracy should mean

- Correct object mentions: Mention objects that are genuinely visible, not objects that are merely common in that environment.
- Coverage of relevant items: Include important, clearly visible objects rather than describing only the most prominent item. Not every tiny or irrelevant object needs to be spoken in every summary.
- Correct relationships: If a laptop is on a desk, describe that relationship only when supported by the image. Do not confuse objects that overlap in the image with objects that physically touch.
- Correct spatial language: Distinguish left, centre and right in the camera view. Use “near” or “far” only when there is a reliable basis for the estimate.
- No invented details: Do not infer hidden objects, people's intentions, exact distances, unseen room layouts or actions that cannot be established from a still frame.
- Useful prioritisation: Start with the main scene and important visible items, then add relevant details. Avoid a long list of every detected object.
- Consistent descriptions: Repeated descriptions of an unchanged scene should not contradict one another without a visual reason.
- Current-frame grounding: Every description must be generated from the current image or a clearly identified recent frame, never from an old description presented as new.

### Recommended measurable targets

Scene description is more complex to evaluate than basic object detection, so use a combination of human-reviewed tests and automated metrics.

| Metric                           | Suggested target                                                                                   |
| -------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Object-mention precision         | At least 95% of specific object claims are supported by the image                                  |
| Important-object coverage        | At least 90% of predefined important visible objects are mentioned in the detailed evaluation mode |
| Spatial relationship correctness | At least 90% on a labelled set of test scenes                                                      |
| Unsupported claims               | Zero fabricated claims in the acceptance-test set                                                  |
| Response time                    | Aim for a first useful description within 1–2 seconds on supported hardware                        |

“Zero fabricated claims” is an acceptance-test goal, not a promise that a probabilistic vision model can never hallucinate. Test results must be reported honestly.

## 5. Shared requirements for all four features

Actual camera evidence

All detections, target locations, OCR results and scene descriptions must be grounded in genuine frames from the camera or explicitly supplied images.

Speed without stale results

Load models once, avoid overlapping inference requests, prioritise fresh frames and measure processing time separately from speech time.

100% sensitivity by default

Initialise the setting to 100% on first load and after reopening or refreshing. Verify that the setting actually changes the detection pipeline. It does not mean 100% accuracy or a 100% confidence threshold.

Real OpenCV 5 integration

Verify the installed OpenCV version in the actual runtime and confirm that real camera frames pass through the intended OpenCV processing stages. Preserve coordinate mappings and compare accuracy and latency with preprocessing enabled and disabled.

Reliable spoken feedback

Speech must describe actual results, avoid excessive repetition, stop when cancelled and never speak stale or fabricated information.

## 6. How to prove the accuracy is real

Test all four features using the same carefully labelled collection of images and videos, with additional feature-specific tests.

- Easy conditions: Clear, well-lit scenes with unobstructed objects.
- Difficult conditions: Low light, glare, motion blur, clutter, small items, occlusion and overlapping objects.
- Different viewpoints: Near the image edges, rotated objects, different camera angles and mirrored previews.
- Negative cases: Requested objects that are absent, unreadable text and scenes where a detail cannot be established.
- Repeatability: Re-run the same tests after code changes to detect regressions.

Report actual measured precision, recall, OCR error rates, scene-grounding results, latency and the hardware/model configuration. Do not substitute a successful demo or a declared package version for measured evidence.

Final standard, bro: OmniLens should detect and describe as many real, supported visible items as possible, identify them correctly, position them correctly, read actual text faithfully, and openly communicate uncertainty. The aim is maximum accuracy for all four features—not merely more boxes on the screen.